---
// Custom illustration set for "Three steps to the seat".
//
// The through-line is ONE wave crossing all three columns. Each panel shares
// the same viewBox and enters at the height its neighbour left off, on a
// horizontal tangent, so the seam between columns is invisible — matching the
// height alone still leaves a visible corner where the slopes disagree. Each
// path also overshoots its own box (x from -10 to 250) so the stroke bridges
// the grid gutter instead of stopping at the column edge; `overflow:visible`
// is what allows it, and the horizontal ends mean the two sides meet at the
// same height and the same slope whatever the column width.
//
// ONE cubic per panel, edge to edge. No H segments, no plateaus: an earlier
// version held a long flat, turned inside a short middle section and held flat
// again, which read as a step rather than a wave. The two S panels are now a
// single Bézier whose control points are mirrored about the midpoint:
//
//   P1 = P0 + (h, 0)        P2 = 2M − P1        M = (P0 + P3) / 2
//
// That construction gives exact 180° rotational symmetry, so the two halves
// are geometric mirrors, the curve passes through M precisely, the inflection
// lands there, and the maximum slope occurs there too and nowhere else (all
// four verified numerically, not by eye). h is a third of the span, the
// canonical smooth S.
//
// The waypoint rings are no longer on a flat, so their cy is the curve's own
// y at x=44 rather than the panel's entry height — 29.8, 14 and 28. Move a
// control point and those have to be recomputed or the ring floats off the
// line. The fly panel is deliberately NOT symmetric; see below.
//
// Below the wave, every panel is built the same way:
//
//   waypoint (coral, x=44)  →  dotted tether straight down  →  the motif
//
// and every motif spans the same band (x 14–210, y 60–102) so the three carry
// equal weight and line up as a row. The tethers differ in length only because
// the wave is at a different height in each panel — they hang like plumb lines.
//
// Each motif depicts its OWN step rather than gesturing at it: a program
// selector with a chosen row, money crossing off the site, an aircraft on a
// route. An earlier pass used abstract bars, a shield and an arrowhead, which
// read as a skeleton loader, "secure" and "growth" respectively — none of them
// said choose / pay / fly.
//
// Two colours only, both from the brand: cobalt carries structure, coral marks
// the one live thing per scene. Every value is a CSS custom property, so the
// art moves with the tokens instead of being pinned to hex.
interface Props { step: "choose" | "pay" | "fly" }
const { step } = Astro.props;
---
{step === "choose" && (
  <svg class="illo" viewBox="0 0 240 110" role="img" aria-label="Choosing a program from the list">
    <path class="il-line" d="M-10 32C77 32 163 12 250 12" />
    <circle class="il-ring" cx="44" cy="29.8" r="10" />
    <circle class="il-dot" cx="44" cy="29.8" r="4.5" />
    <path class="il-tether" d="M44 41.8V58" />

    <!-- a program list with one row chosen: carrier chip, name, rate -->
    <rect class="il-chip" x="20" y="62" width="13" height="9" rx="3" />
    <rect class="il-bar" x="41" y="65" width="72" height="4" rx="2" />
    <rect class="il-bar-dim" x="121" y="65" width="26" height="4" rx="2" />

    <rect class="il-chip-on" x="20" y="77" width="13" height="9" rx="3" />
    <rect class="il-bar-on" x="41" y="80" width="86" height="4" rx="2" />
    <rect class="il-rate" x="135" y="80" width="30" height="4" rx="2" />

    <rect class="il-chip" x="20" y="92" width="13" height="9" rx="3" />
    <rect class="il-bar" x="41" y="95" width="58" height="4" rx="2" />
    <rect class="il-bar-dim" x="107" y="95" width="26" height="4" rx="2" />
  </svg>
)}

{step === "pay" && (
  <svg class="illo" viewBox="0 0 240 110" role="img" aria-label="Payment handled away from this site">
    <path class="il-line" d="M-10 12C77 12 163 30 250 30" />
    <circle class="il-ring" cx="44" cy="14" r="10" />
    <circle class="il-dot" cx="44" cy="14" r="4.5" />
    <path class="il-tether" d="M44 26V58" />

    <!-- Money, and it leaves. A note rather than a coin: two concentric circles
         read as a target or a record button, not as payment. The arrow leads
         away and keeps going, because nothing is ever charged here. -->
    <rect class="il-note" x="13" y="62" width="62" height="32" rx="6" />
    <circle class="il-note-in" cx="44" cy="78" r="7.5" />

    <path class="il-cross" d="M89 78h111" />
    <path class="il-arrow" d="M198 72l11 6-11 6z" />
  </svg>
)}

{step === "fly" && (
  <svg class="illo" viewBox="0 0 240 110" role="img" aria-label="The seat booked and the aircraft away">
    <!-- Last panel, so the wave terminates here rather than overshooting into a
         column that doesn't exist, and it ends in an arrowhead rotated onto the
         path's own end tangent so the head continues the line instead of
         sitting at an angle to it.

         That departure angle is the logo's. The brand mark climbs 34° from the
         start of its stroke to the destination ring, and it does so by
         accelerating: nearly flat at the start, steepest at the end. This curve
         is shaped the same way and leaves at the same 34°, so the one moment on
         the page that depicts flying away reads as the mark does. The earlier
         version flattened into its arrowhead at 5°, which decelerated exactly
         where the logo lifts. Entry stays pinned at the height the panel
         before it left off, on a horizontal tangent — that seam is not
         negotiable.

         So this panel is the one exception to the mirrored-control-point rule
         above: a departure is asymmetric by definition. It still runs edge to
         edge as a single cubic with no flat lead-in. -->
    <path class="il-line" d="M-10 30C60 30 186 15.7 199 7" />
    <g class="il-tip" transform="translate(199 7) rotate(-34)">
      <path d="M-3 -6.5L12 0-3 6.5z" />
    </g>
    <circle class="il-ring" cx="44" cy="28" r="10" />
    <circle class="il-dot" cx="44" cy="28" r="4.5" />
    <path class="il-tether" d="M44 40V58" />

    <!-- the aircraft hangs plumb under its waypoint: its centre is x=44, the
         same x the ring and tether use, like the note in the panel before -->
    <g class="il-plane" transform="translate(44 78) rotate(40) scale(.95)">
      <path d="M0 -22c2.2 0 4 3.4 4 7.6V-8l16 9.6v5.2L4 2v9.6l6 4.4v3.6L0 17.2l-10 2.4V16l6-4.4V2l-16 4.8V1.6l16-9.6v-6.4c0-4.2 1.8-7.6 4-7.6z" />
    </g>
  </svg>
)}

<style>
  .illo { display:block; width:100%; height:auto; margin-bottom:20px; overflow:visible; }
  /* Below this the columns are too narrow to draw the wave legibly, and the
     steps run as a compact three-up text row instead. Scoped here rather than
     in flightdeck.css because Astro's scoping makes the rule above and a
     `.steps .illo` override equally specific — same-specificity ties go to
     whichever loads last, which is this file. */
  @media (max-width:820px){ .illo { display:none; } }

  /* the route — one unbroken stroke the whole way across */
  .il-line { fill:none; stroke:var(--cobalt); stroke-width:2.5; stroke-linecap:round; }

  /* the waypoint, and the tether down to this step's motif */
  .il-ring   { fill:none; stroke:var(--cobalt-line); stroke-width:2.5; }
  .il-dot    { fill:var(--coral); }
  .il-tether { fill:none; stroke:var(--cobalt-line); stroke-width:2.5; stroke-linecap:round; stroke-dasharray:1 6; }

  /* step 1 — the program list */
  .il-chip    { fill:var(--mist); }
  .il-chip-on { fill:var(--cobalt); }
  .il-bar     { fill:var(--mist); }
  .il-bar-dim { fill:var(--line-2); }
  .il-bar-on  { fill:var(--cobalt); }
  .il-rate    { fill:var(--coral); }

  /* step 2 — money crossing off the site */
  .il-note     { fill:var(--cobalt-tint); stroke:var(--cobalt); stroke-width:2.5; }
  .il-note-in  { fill:none; stroke:var(--cobalt); stroke-width:2.5; }
  .il-cross    { fill:none; stroke:var(--coral); stroke-width:2.5; stroke-linecap:round; }
  .il-arrow    { fill:var(--coral); }

  /* step 3 — the route and the aircraft */
  .il-plane  { fill:var(--cobalt); }
  .il-tip    { fill:var(--cobalt); }
</style>
